{
  "content": "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the task spec from the full user trace, cumulatively\nTreat every user turn as additive evidence. Build one merged specification from all user messages only, and update it whenever later user text clarifies, extends, or overrides earlier ambiguity. Do not freeze the first plausible interpretation.\n\n### 2) Let later user messages resolve ambiguity and complete the task\nIf an earlier turn is underspecified, mark it as incomplete rather than wrong. Once a later turn supplies the missing fact, treat the task as determinate and retroactively retire earlier \u201cneeds more info\u201d stances, speculative branches, or placeholder reasoning that no longer fit.\n\n### 3) Recompute whenever a new user shard changes a binding constraint\nDo not treat later details as commentary if they alter a total, current amount, base quantity, reference group, comparison relation, or endpoint. When a user update changes the state, recompute against the updated constraint set instead of extending an obsolete partial answer.\n\n### 4) Separate temporary incompleteness from final divergence\nEarly partial reasoning can be aligned if the user has not yet provided enough information. But once later turns complete the spec, re-evaluate whether that earlier stance became inconsistent. The analyzer should distinguish \u201cnot enough yet\u201d from \u201cultimately wrong after the full trace.\u201d\n\n### 5) Anchor on the full constraint graph, not a local calculation\nA locally correct intermediate result can still fail if it conflicts with later user facts. Validate the assistant\u2019s behavior against the entire merged specification, not only the latest equation or the most recent turn in isolation.\n\n### 6) Preserve relational structure exactly\nPhrases like \u201cof the remainder,\u201d \u201cof those,\u201d \u201ctogether,\u201d \u201cshared equally,\u201d \u201ctwice as many as,\u201d or \u201cper client\u201d encode structural relations, not loose wording. The analyzer should keep these dependencies explicit; flattening a linked quantity into a standalone number is a common source of divergence.\n\n### 7) Track state deltas and running totals explicitly\nWhen users add, subtract, replace, or otherwise update a quantity, map the new information to the current state immediately. Check whether the latest turn is modifying an existing total or introducing a new one, and verify that the assistant\u2019s reasoning consumed that update rather than stopping at the prior subtotal.\n\n### 8) Distinguish current state from final answer\nA correct intermediate total is not a final solution if later user turns continue the same ledger, count, or rate relation. Flag responses that stop at an in-progress state as incomplete even when the arithmetic up to that point is correct.\n\n### 9) Verify the assistant against all disclosed facts, especially \u201camount needed\u201d vs \u201camount already available\u201d\nOne frequent failure mode is solving for the target total while ignoring what is already on hand, already used, or already established. Make sure the assistant\u2019s result reconciles with both the goal quantity and any current quantity or accumulated amount the user has already provided.\n\n### 10) Watch for silent base shifts and unit reinterpretations\nFlag cases where the assistant reinterprets a total as a per-unit value, changes the reference group, or rescales a quantity without support from the user. Many divergences come from changing the base quantity while acting as if nothing changed.\n\n### 11) Use direct user-stated relations over inferred patterns\nIf the user\u2019s own facts already determine the answer, direct computation should outrank any inferred sequence, template, or repeated pattern. Treat unsupported extrapolation as overreach when the full user trace already pins down the task.\n\n### 12) Flag invented assumptions that fill ambiguity without support\nIf the assistant introduces a subtype, hidden split, default rule, or interpretation not grounded in the user\u2019s text, mark it as speculative. This matters most when later user messages show that the extra assumption was unnecessary or wrong. Diagnose the exact unsupported assumption, not just the final bad output.\n\n### 13) Diagnose with the first wrong assumption that cascades\nWhen multiple downstream errors appear, identify the earliest user-misread constraint that forces them. The most useful explanation is often the initial misunderstanding of quantity, base, relation, or scope that made later reasoning drift.\n\n### 14) Separate scope mismatch from arithmetic error\nA correct computation of the wrong question is still a failure. If the assistant answers an earlier, narrower, or adjacent task instead of the final user request, flag a scope mismatch even when the math is valid.\n\n### 15) Preserve only reasoning that survives the latest update\nWhen the user revises the task, keep intermediate steps that still hold under the merged spec and discard conclusions tied to superseded facts. Compact context should retain validated state and remove stale branches so later analysis starts from the true current task.\n\n### 16) Treat the latest user turn as a completion signal when it closes the loop\nIf the final user message supplies the decisive fact, transformation, or constraint, the analyzer should expect resolution rather than more probing. Check whether the assistant failed to conclude after the task became answer-ready.\n\n### 17) Use exact final-state consistency checks for chained disclosures\nFor multi-turn conversations where each user turn changes the same state, verify that all user statements can coexist in one coherent end-state. The key question is whether the final state can be derived from the full trace, not whether each individual turn seemed plausible in isolation.\n\n### 18) Keep contextual flavor secondary to operative constraints\nNarrative framing, roles, or domain-specific color matter only if they change the required output or the binding relations. Do not let story context override explicit numeric updates, corrections, or late clarifications.\n\n### 19) In diagnostics, name the missed user detail and its effect\nIn \u201cWhat Needs to Change,\u201d be specific about the user statement that was ignored or misread and why it changes the result. Prefer explanations like \u201cfailed to incorporate the later update to the running total\u201d or \u201cmisread the fixed total as a current amount\u201d over generic labels like \u201cincorrect reasoning.\u201d\n\n### 20) Prefer incremental reconciliation over restarting the problem\nWhen new user information arrives, update the existing state rather than re-deriving from an obsolete partial interpretation. This helps avoid anchoring on an earlier branch and preserves correct intermediate results that still survive the update.",
  "version": 4,
  "history": [
    "",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the task spec cumulatively, not locally\nTreat every new user message as a potential constraint update. Reconstruct the full task specification from all user turns before judging the assistant, and revalidate earlier assumptions whenever later disclosures add, refine, or contradict facts. Don\u2019t evaluate against a partial earlier version once the user has supplied a more complete one.\n\n### 2) Prefer explicit user facts over inferred placeholders\nWhen the user gives a concrete value, correction, or clarifying relation, it replaces earlier guesses, placeholders, or derived assumptions. Collapse unresolved quantities as soon as the missing information appears; do not continue treating them as unknown if the user has already resolved them. A later correction should reset the affected subproblem rather than layering on top of prior speculation.\n\n### 3) Separate stated facts from inferred structure\nDistinguish what the user explicitly said from what the assistant inferred. If the user names a subset, remainder, reference quantity, or comparative relation, keep that relation explicit instead of flattening it into a single count. Common failure mode: the assistant silently converts an \u201cof the remainder / of the people who are X / twice as many as Y\u201d statement into an unrelated standalone quantity.\n\n### 4) Look for partition, remainder, and overlap signals\nPhrases like \u201cremaining,\u201d \u201cof those,\u201d \u201csubset of,\u201d \u201ctwice as many as,\u201d or \u201ctogether\u201d often signal that quantities are linked by inclusion, complement, or aggregation. The analyzer should verify whether the assistant respected those links or prematurely treated one group as the whole. Check conservation across groups: do the parts still sum or reconcile with the total after all user disclosures?\n\n### 5) Detect contradiction-by-assumption\nFlag it when the assistant invents a hidden subtype split, workaround, or interpretation that the user never confirmed, especially if later user text provides an explicit aggregate or total-time statement that makes the invented split unnecessary or wrong. If the assistant fixes an ambiguity on its own while the user\u2019s wording allows multiple readings, mark that as risky or incorrect rather than accepted.\n\n### 6) Track \u201cwhat is solved\u201d vs \u201cwhat remains unaccounted for\u201d\nA correct intermediate result can still be incomplete. The analyzer should preserve valid subresults while explicitly checking whether later user turns introduced additional required pieces that were never incorporated into the final synthesis. Use this to catch cases where the assistant stops after one subgroup, one stage of a process, or an intermediate total.\n\n### 7) Recompute from the full accumulated state when later turns change the denominator or target\nIf a later user message modifies the base quantity, reference group, or denominator of a percentage/ratio/total calculation, the earlier arithmetic path may no longer apply. Re-evaluate the final answer from the original full task state, not from an intermediate remainder or already-partial total that has been superseded.\n\n### 8) Diagnose stale problem framing\nA common failure is that the assistant answers the earlier version of the problem even after the user has updated it. Check whether the assistant\u2019s latest reasoning is anchored to an obsolete interpretation, especially in sharded conversations where the user adds missing context over multiple turns.\n\n### 9) Distinguish contextual flavor from operative constraints\nRole descriptions, story details, and scene-setting text may be irrelevant unless they change the requested output or constraints. Do not let narrative context override direct user instructions, numerical relationships, or explicit corrections. Use context only when it genuinely affects the task spec.\n\n### 10) Build compact, actionable diagnostics\nWhen flagging an issue, identify the missing linkage, missing constraint, or wrong interpretation that changed the outcome. Prefer \u201ccorrect subresult, incomplete final synthesis\u201d or \u201cignored later correction to the reference quantity\u201d over vague labels. The best diagnosis tells the next step what state must be restored, not how to solve the underlying task.\n\n### 11) For rewrite/compaction steps, keep only validated state\nWhen preparing an S2-style compacted context, retain the confirmed totals, confirmed relations, and any unresolved references that still matter. Drop speculative branches and misleading intermediate conclusions, even if their local arithmetic was right, if they would anchor the next response to the wrong interpretation.\n\n### 12) Treat each new shard as refinement of one evolving problem\nIn multi-turn conversations, later user shards usually refine the same task rather than introducing separate tasks. Merge them into one evolving specification, then re-check whether the assistant\u2019s answer remains consistent with the updated state. If the new shard changes the meaning of an earlier number or comparison, the earlier conclusion should be reconsidered, not preserved by inertia.",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Reconstruct the task spec cumulatively from user-only content\nTreat every user message as a potential constraint update. Build a single evolving specification from all user turns before judging the assistant, and re-check earlier assumptions whenever later user text refines, overrides, or completes them. Do not let early assistant guesses become part of the spec.\n\n### 2) Judge the assistant at the disclosure point, then revisit after later shards\nFor any given turn, evaluate only against the information available up to that moment. If the assistant pauses for missing information, that can be aligned at that point; if later user messages supply the missing pieces, re-assess whether the earlier \u201cneeds more info\u201d stance became obsolete. Distinguish \u201ctemporarily incomplete\u201d from \u201cultimately wrong.\u201d\n\n### 3) Treat later user details as decisive, not additive noise\nWhen a later user shard adds a multiplier, scope, aggregate, equation, or target change, test whether it changes the total problem, not just a local substep. Recompute the full implied state from the updated constraint set rather than preserving earlier numeric conclusions by inertia.\n\n### 4) Separate intermediate results from the requested output\nA correct subtotal, partial derivation, or local relation is not enough if the user asked for a final aggregate or a different derived quantity. Track what is solved versus what remains required, and flag cases where the assistant presents a valid intermediate result as if it completed the task.\n\n### 5) Keep explicit relational structure intact\nDo not flatten user statements like \u201cof the remainder,\u201d \u201cof those,\u201d \u201csubset of,\u201d \u201ctogether,\u201d \u201cshared equally,\u201d or \u201ctwice as many as\u201d into standalone quantities. These phrases encode inclusion, complement, overlap, or distribution constraints that must remain explicit in the task model. Common error: the assistant computes with a count that ignores the relation tying it to another group.\n\n### 6) Watch for overlap, remainder, and conservation failures\nWhen categories can intersect or parts sum to a total, check whether the assistant respected the necessary accounting. Diagnose whether the error is missing a total, missing an overlap, or failing to conserve the whole across parts. A good local count can still be wrong if the full partition no longer reconciles.\n\n### 7) Re-solve when the denominator, reference group, or base changes\nIf a later user turn changes the base quantity, comparison group, or denominator of a ratio/percentage/average-like statement, earlier arithmetic may no longer apply. Force a full re-evaluation from the accumulated user constraints instead of extending an obsolete intermediate calculation.\n\n### 8) Flag invented assumptions and hidden splits\nTreat assistant-introduced subtypes, workaround interpretations, or unconfirmed splits as suspect unless the user explicitly supported them. If the assistant resolves ambiguity on its own while the wording still allows multiple readings, classify that as a risky assumption or divergence, especially when later user text makes the added assumption unnecessary.\n\n### 9) Distinguish scope mismatch from arithmetic error\nIf the assistant answers a different question than the one the user ultimately asked, mark a scope mismatch even if the calculation itself is correct. Use this when the assistant solves an earlier or narrower version of the problem, stops at an intermediate target, or misses a late-breaking clarification that changes the requested output.\n\n### 10) Preserve aligned subresults, discard unsupported anchoring\nWhen compacting context or explaining divergence, keep validated equations, confirmed totals, and resolved relations, but remove speculative branches and stale conclusions that would anchor later reasoning to the wrong model. Prefer \u201creplace and compact\u201d behavior when new user shards revise the problem framing.\n\n### 11) Diagnose with exact omitted constraints and exact consequence\nThe most useful \u201cwhat needs to change\u201d notes name the missing user detail and its effect on the outcome. Prefer diagnostics like \u201cmissed later aggregate constraint that changes the final total\u201d or \u201cignored the user\u2019s refinement of the reference group\u201d over vague labels such as \u201cincorrect reasoning.\u201d The goal is to identify the broken linkage, not to prescribe a full solution.\n\n### 12) Treat the final user turn as a possible completion signal\nUse the latest user message to check whether the task has become fully specified. If earlier turns looked incomplete but the last shard supplies the missing quantity, relation, or operation, update the spec immediately and reassess prior partial answers under the complete state.\n\n### 13) Use task-state consistency as the main diagnostic lens\nThe key question is whether the assistant\u2019s output still satisfies the full set of user-stated constraints accumulated so far. Prefer this over judging explanation quality or local plausibility. A conversation can sound coherent while still failing the combined constraint system.\n\n### 14) Separate contextual flavor from operative constraints\nStory details, roles, and narrative framing matter only if they change the required output or constraints. Do not let contextual color override direct user instructions, explicit corrections, or numerical relations. Use context only when it genuinely alters the specification.\n\n### 15) For sharded conversations, compare against one merged constraint graph\nDo not analyze each turn in isolation. Merge all user-stated facts into a single constraint graph, then check whether the assistant\u2019s reasoning and final answer satisfy that graph. This is the most reliable way to catch stale framing, missed late constraints, and premature finality.",
    "**Updated cheatsheet**\n\n### 1) Rebuild the task spec cumulatively from all user messages only\nTreat each user turn as a possible constraint update, not as isolated context. Merge the full user history into one evolving specification before judging the assistant, and revise earlier assumptions whenever later user text refines, overrides, or completes the task.\n\n### 2) Use the latest user turn as the active spec when it resolves ambiguity\nIf the final user message supplies missing facts, clarifies a relation, or changes the target quantity, treat that as the authoritative version of the task. Retroactively mark earlier \u201cneeds more info\u201d stances, placeholder formulas, or speculative branches as obsolete once the spec is complete.\n\n### 3) Re-evaluate the whole task when a new user shard changes any binding constraint\nDo not treat later details as mere narrative additions. If a new user statement changes a total, base quantity, denominator, reference group, endpoint, or comparison relation, recompute the implied state from the updated constraint set instead of extending an obsolete partial solution.\n\n### 4) Distinguish temporary incompleteness from final divergence\nAt an early turn, \u201ccannot answer yet\u201d or partial reasoning may be aligned if the user has not supplied enough information. But once later shards complete the task, re-check whether that earlier stance became wrong. The analyzer should separate \u201ctemporarily incomplete\u201d from \u201cultimately inconsistent with the merged spec.\u201d\n\n### 5) Anchor on the full constraint graph, not the latest local formula\nThe main diagnostic question is whether the assistant still satisfies the entire merged user constraint set. A locally correct intermediate calculation can still fail if it conflicts with a later total, boundary condition, or relation. Compare outputs against the whole conversation state, not just the last equation mentioned.\n\n### 6) Preserve relational structure exactly\nTreat phrases like \u201cof the remainder,\u201d \u201cof those,\u201d \u201ctogether,\u201d \u201cshared equally,\u201d \u201ctwice as many as,\u201d or \u201cper client\u201d as structural constraints, not loose wording. These phrases define inclusion, overlap, distribution, or rate relationships that must remain explicit in the task model. A common failure is flattening a linked quantity into a standalone number and losing the relationship that drives the answer.\n\n### 7) Watch for silent unit shifts and base changes\nFlag cases where the assistant reinterprets a total as a per-unit value, changes a reference group, or rescales a quantity without user support. If the user has already fixed the meaning of a total or rate, later arithmetic must respect that meaning. Many divergences come from changing the base quantity while acting as if nothing changed.\n\n### 8) Recompute when later facts affect the whole state, not just a local step\nIf the user adds a final aggregate, ending state, cumulative total, or comparison condition, verify that the earlier arithmetic still reconciles with the full chain. When the latest user message closes the loop, the analyzer should rebuild the state from the newest terminal facts backward rather than anchoring on earlier partial results.\n\n### 9) Separate aligned intermediate results from unsupported final conclusions\nAn assistant may correctly compute subtotals, intermediate relations, or partial derivations and still fail by answering the wrong target, stopping too early, or extrapolating beyond the evidence. Preserve validated subresults, but isolate the step where the reasoning first departs from user-supported constraints.\n\n### 10) Flag invented assumptions, especially when they fill ambiguity without user support\nIf the assistant introduces a subtype, hidden split, pattern rule, or interpretation not explicitly grounded in the user\u2019s text, treat it as speculative. This is especially important when the wording still permits multiple readings or when later user text shows the extra assumption was unnecessary. The core issue is not \u201cguessing\u201d in general, but guessing a binding constraint.\n\n### 11) Diagnose with the exact invalidated assumption and its consequence\nIn the \u201cWhat Needs to Change\u201d section, name the specific user detail that was missed or misread and the reason it changes the result. Prefer diagnostics like \u201cignored the later aggregate constraint that changes the final total\u201d or \u201cmisread the fixed total as a per-unit amount\u201d over vague labels such as \u201cincorrect reasoning.\u201d\n\n### 12) Distinguish scope mismatch from arithmetic error\nIf the assistant solves an earlier, narrower, or adjacent problem instead of the final user request, mark a scope mismatch even when the math is valid. This includes answering an intermediate quantity when the user asked for the final outcome, or failing to update the target after a late clarification. A correct computation of the wrong question is still a failure.\n\n### 13) Prefer recomputation over preserving stale reasoning once the spec is complete\nWhen later user shards make the problem fully specified, the analyzer should shift from \u201cneeds clarification\u201d to full validation. Do not let earlier speculative branches continue to influence the evaluation after the task has become solvable. Use compacted context that retains only aligned facts and discards superseded interpretations.\n\n### 14) Use exact final-state consistency checks for chained disclosures\nFor multi-turn problems where each turn changes the same ledger, total, or rate relation, verify that every disclosed fact can coexist with the others. The useful check is whether the final state can be derived consistently from all user statements together, not whether each individual turn seems plausible on its own.\n\n### 15) Treat contextual flavor as secondary to operative constraints\nNarrative framing, roles, or other story details matter only if they change the required output or the binding constraints. Do not let context color override explicit numeric relations, corrections, or late clarifications. Use framing only when it genuinely alters the task specification.\n\n### 16) Look for the first wrong assumption that cascades\nWhen the assistant makes multiple downstream mistakes, identify the earliest user-misread constraint that forces later errors. The most useful diagnosis is often not the final arithmetic slip but the initial misinterpretation of the quantity, base, or relation that made the rest of the reasoning drift.\n\n### 17) Preserve only aligned reasoning that survives the update\nWhen the user updates the task, keep the assistant\u2019s reasoning steps that still hold under the new merged specification, and discard any intermediate conclusions tied to superseded facts. This helps the analyzer explain divergence without anchoring on stale but plausible subresults.\n\n### 18) Use direct user-stated relations over inferred patterns\nIf the user has provided enough information to determine the answer directly, that direct computation should outrank any inferred sequence, template, or pattern continuation. Treat unsupported pattern extrapolation as overreach when the user\u2019s own facts already determine the target."
